1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
50% chance of 10,000 and a 50% chance of zero.

2
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:09,940
So this is one ticket.

3
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:12,000
That's one lottery ticket that I have.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,360
Gives me 50% chance of 10,000, 50% chance of zero.

5
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:17,240
Or I could have a different ticket.

6
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,920
I have another ticket that has 100% probability

7
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:21,160
of giving me 2,000.

8
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:23,700
Different ticket.

9
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:26,480
All right, do you want to have this ticket or that ticket?

10
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:29,320
Well, so inspecting utility theory says

11
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:32,760
that you do or act as if you do,

12
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:41,320
is that you go and take the probability

13
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:46,320
of the first outcome, 50%, times the value of that.

14
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:49,840
So that's going to be square root of 10,000,

15
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:53,440
plus.5 times the square root of zero.

16
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:55,600
And you're going to compare that to,

17
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:57,800
so greater equal less than,

18
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:01,960
to one times the square root of 2,000.

19
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:06,800
So notice, we've got the value function,

20
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:09,180
which comes in here, here, and here,

21
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:10,760
where we just take the square root of the wealth.

22
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:12,580
And again, this is just reflecting

23
00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:14,320
our attitude towards risk.

24
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:17,520
But then when we go and put in the probabilities,

25
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:18,820
we don't do anything special with those.

26
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:20,600
We just put those in linearly.

27
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:23,400
50% chance means we multiply it by.5.

28
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:25,580
100% means we multiply it by one.

29
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:28,360
One in 1,000, then we multiply it by.001.

30
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:31,520
And then we just compare.

31
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:33,120
And if we take a look, then we'll see

32
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:36,720
that this has an expected utility of 50,

33
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:38,560
for the first one, that's 50.

34
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:43,560
This has an expected utility of 44.72.

35
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:45,440
So we choose the higher one.

36
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:47,520
The higher one is 50.

37
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:50,600
So given these assumptions, we will choose this.

38
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:55,500
So that is the procedure.

39
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:56,600
That is the procedure.

40
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:01,300
Now, there's something very important about this,

41
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:03,260
which is easy to overlook.

42
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:04,680
Namely, people first hear about this,

43
00:02:04,680 --> 00:02:07,600
and they say, of course, duh, everybody does this.

44
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:10,080
There's a lot of evidence people don't do this either.

45
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:10,960
How so?

46
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:15,400
Well, suppose that you're moving from a 1% chance

47
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:17,760
of a bad outcome happening to a 0%.

48
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,520
In, with expected utility theory,

49
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:21,360
this is just gonna be a,

50
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:23,340
this'll have the same little change

51
00:02:23,340 --> 00:02:26,800
as moving from 2% to 1%.

52
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:30,320
And yet, normal human beings seem to really like

53
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:33,600
going from 1% chance of something bad to 0%,

54
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:34,880
but don't seem to care very much

55
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:37,360
about going from 2% to 1%.

56
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:39,040
According to expected utility theory,

57
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:42,000
moving from 2% to 1% is just as valuable

58
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:43,320
as going from one to zero.

59
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:46,640
According to normal human beings, not so.

60
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:48,840
That 1% really bothers them.

61
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:50,680
But you say, hey, I can reduce your risk

62
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:52,640
of a really bad thing by one percentage point.

63
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:55,400
It's like, well, how much is the risk?

64
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:56,920
Like, well, what's the difference?

65
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:58,760
It's like, well, if it goes from one to zero,

66
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:00,400
then I can finally sleep easy,

67
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:03,320
knowing there's a 0% chance of a bad thing happening.

68
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:05,800
If it goes from two to one, who cares?

69
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:07,040
Well, you should care,

70
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:08,960
because it's a one percentage point reduction

71
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:10,760
in a terrible thing happening to you.

72
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:16,680
All right, so that is one of the most unrealistic things

73
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:18,200
about expected utility theory.

74
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:20,560
At the same time, it's one of the main things

75
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:23,240
where if you take it seriously, you might repent and say,

76
00:03:23,240 --> 00:03:25,680
other human beings are crazy, but not me.

77
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:28,600
I'm gonna start worrying about moving probabilities

78
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:31,600
from one to 0% more than moving them from 2% to 1%.

79
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:35,360
Now, if you think about the way that people deal

80
